<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Sultanate of Tsibiri</h1><p class="st-meta">1835 CE – 1899 CE (approx.) · capital Tsibiri</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The refuge state of the Hausa rulers of Gobir in southern Niger, which kept up the war against the Sokoto Caliphate for most of the 19th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gobir">Kingdom of Gobir</a> was the first target of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>, and its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alkalawa">Alkalawa</a> fell in 1808. Its ruling family survived in exile, and in the 1830s they established a new capital at Tsibiri, north of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>, in alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> exiles of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-maradi">Sultanate of Maradi</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Tsibiri preserved the titles and traditions of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gobir">Gobir</a> and acted as a base for raids into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>. In 1836 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-maradi">Maradi</a> forces were defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bello">Muhammad Bello</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gawakuke">battle of Gawakuke</a>, and the Gobir ruler was killed, but the war continued for decades. Tsibiri's rulers were Muslims, yet they opposed the political authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>French forces occupied the area in 1899, and Tsibiri was absorbed into the territory that became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-niger">Colony of Niger</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gobir">Gobir</a> still reigns at Tsibiri as a traditional ruler.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="sultanate, 1835 CE – 1899 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">sultanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.22%;width:1.27%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1835 CE – 1899 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tsibiri</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hausa</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/niger">Niger</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gobir-Tsibiri, Tsibiri</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1867&amp;focus=sultanate-of-tsibiri" aria-label="Sultanate of Tsibiri on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 1695 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sultanate of Tsibiri lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5174 1951L5172 1927M5174 1951L5058 1930"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5172 1927h0M5058 1930h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5174 1951h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5174 1951h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tsibiri. <a href="/atlas?year=1867&amp;focus=sultanate-of-tsibiri">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
